      {
        "title": "Zechariah's Futuristic Prophecies",
        "introduction": "There is no indication at all as to when the final prophecies of Zechariah are either received or recorded. It is not likely that they would have come later than about 460 B.C. Since the historical record is otherwise virtually silent for about 35 years, Zechariah's last prophecies will be set forth at this point."
      },
      {
        "introduction": "The prophecies, in the form of two oracles, are once again highly figurative. They are undoubtedly messianic and give some of the clearest details about the life and death of the Messiah. For example, in contrast to the present kings and their chariots of war, this spiritual King will come gently in peace, riding on a donkey. He will be betrayed for 30 pieces of silver, and he will be pierced at the time of his death.\n\nZechariah addresses a number of themes—past, present, and future—which lead to an apocalyptic view of the great battle between good and evil, the destruction of the wicked, and the salvation of the righteous."
      },
